description:
The objective of this procurement is to implement a fit-for-purpose, trauma informed, culturally safe wellbeing support service available to:
provide pre-engagement, on the day psychological first aid/grounding, and limited post-engagement debriefs and referrals;
reduce the risk of distress, re-traumatisation, and safety incidents;
support safe participation for diverse groups, including people with service experience in the Australian Defence Force, people with disability, Aboriginal and Torress Strait Islander peoples, culturally and linguistically diverse (CALD) particpants, LGBTQIA+ participants, young people, and others with specific needs; and
integrate with engagement operations while safeguarding confidentiality, privacy, and the independence of the Defence and Veterans' Service Commission.

The services will comprise of two complementary but distinct service streams, with separate delivery, financial management and performance reporting arrangements for each program. DFAT is seeking to secure a flexible, responsive and participant-centered provider to:
• deliver accessible information and practical support to participants from eligible Pacific countries and Timor-Leste on ballot, visa, employment, pre-departure and settlement stages to make informed decisions, according to the scope of each service stream;
• connect participants with suitable and timely employment opportunities, recognising that a formal job offer is a PEV visa requirement while FMP employment support is focused on successful settlement and economic participation;
• deliver enhanced, culturally grounded offshore (in Tuvalu) and onshore (in Australia) support for FMP participants consistent with mobility with dignity and the bilateral significance of the Falepili Union Treaty;
• collect and report regular, timely, high-quality, quantitative and qualitative disaggregated program and participant data to support performance monitoring, decision-making and continuous improvement;
• adapt delivery to changing allocations, countries, participant needs and level of demand, policy settings and settlement patterns; and
• support FMP participants and the Tuvaluan diaspora in Australia to maintain ongoing connections to Tuvalu, including cultural identity, language, community networks and links to country, consistent with the commitments of the Falepili Union Treaty.

The Invitation to provide Day Procedure Centre Services to the Veteran Community seeks to ensure that the veteran and Defence Force communities, and their treating doctors, continue to have wide access to high quality stand-alone Day Procedure Centre services.
Eligible providers are invited to submit an Invitation Response Proforma and apply to contract with DVA through a new, simplified arrangement. The key features of the new arrangements are:
an overall objective of maximising the numbers of participating providers, to achieve wide access to DPCs for the veteran community;
there is no formal comparison of responses against each other;
providers are required to submit their fee tables and acknowledge their compliance with the terms and conditions of this Invitation;
providers declare their ability to comply with DVA’s terms and conditions of the DPC Services Agreement;
providers can submit a response and apply to enter into the DPC Services Agreement at any time while this Invitation is available on AusTender;
the Agreement will continue until either party ends the arrangements; and
there is a standard DPC Services Agreement with negotiation only on fees to ensure value for money.

Joint Approach to Market – Office Accommodation in Hobart, Tasmania.
Services Australia, acting on behalf of the Commonwealth and the Participating Entities (the Commonwealth), invites Tenderers to submit Proposals for the provision of contemporary, fit-for-purpose office accommodation in Hobart, Tasmania.
Proposals are invited for accommodation solutions that:
• are located within the Permitted Geographic Area identified in the RFP Statement of Requirement;
• provide approximately 10,000 to 14,400 square metres of Net Lettable Area;
• provide flexibility to increase or decrease the Premises by 15%;
• desirably include a ground-floor component with a minimum area of approximately 830 square metres;
• provide contiguous floor plates within the building footprint;
• provide PCA A-grade accommodation or better;
• comprise an existing building ready for use, an existing building requiring refurbishment, or a new development opportunity; and
• support the Commonwealth’s target Lease Commencement Date of 1 July 2031, or an earlier date by mutual agreement.
A minimum of 43 car parking bays is highly desirable, comprising 41 secure and covered bays, with at least 25% of spaces provided with electric vehicle charging, and two accessible bays at ground level close to the entrance.
The Commonwealth is seeking an initial lease(s) term of 10 years, with two options to renew of three years each.
The Commonwealth will consider Tenderer-delivered, integrated and Commonwealth-delivered fit-out solutions, as further detailed in the RFP Documents.

